window.docContents[79].resolve({"/Overview/#overview":{"contents":"\n\nThis section showcases various features of cvc.lean for new users to get an idea of what it can do. It is light on technical details which are provided in the rest of this manual. For now just know that cvc.lean has two APIs that differ in how Terms (and a few other types) are encoded and the static guarantees they provide:\n\n* Cvc.Typed strongly-types terms so that the cvc5-level sort of a term appears in its type, making ill-formed terms (and most solver queries) fail at compile-times. For instance:* Term Bool, Term String, etc.* asserting a formula in a solver takes a Term Bool.* Cvc.Untyped just has a simple type Term for terms and you are responsible for creating well-formed terms and querying the solver correctly; failure to do so will result in a runtime error.\n\nThe examples below use the typed API.\n\nopen Cvc Typed\n\n\nAlso, we need an instance of type-class Ω in scope to manipulate sorts, terms, solvers, etc.\n\nvariable [Ω]\n\n\nThink of it as a \"cvc5 world\" which we need to do anything, in particular to write code in cvc.lean's Env monad.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5","header":"1. Overview","id":"/Overview/#overview"},"/Solver/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Solver--More-check-sat-functions":{"contents":"For simple check-sat use cases where the only thing that matters is whether the current assertions are satisfiable or not one can simply use Solver.checkIsSat.\n\nChecks the satisfiability of the constraints asserted so far.\n\nOftentimes we want to return a result specific to a sat/unsat result, and nothing otherwise, for instance a model on a sat result and nothing on unsat. In such cases it can be easier to use Solver.checkSat? which lets the caller specify which outcome produces a result and how, the unexpected result producing none by default.On an unknown outcome, the default throws an error instead of returning none as unknown is considered to never be expected by default.\n\nChecks the satisfiability of the constraints asserted so far.\n\n#eval Env.runIO do\n  let b ← Term.mkSymbol \"b\"\n  let i1 ← Term.mkSymbol \"i1\"\n  let i2 ← Term.mkSymbol \"i2\"\n  let t1 ← smt! b ∧ 0 < i1\n  let t2 ← smt! i1 % i2 = i1\n  let solver ← Solver.new\n  solver.setOpt .smt_produceModels true\n  if ← solver.checkIsSat then\n    println! \"sat, adding constraint\"\n    let values? ← solver.checkSat? (assuming := #[t2])\n      (ifSat := return some (← i1.getValue, ← i2.getValue))\n    if let some (i1Val, i2Val) := values?\n    then println! \"  i1 := {i1Val}, i2 := {i2Val}\"\n    else println! \"  unsat with additional constraints\"\n  else println! \"unsat\"\n\n\nsat, adding constraint\n  i1 := 0, i2 := 0\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5\u0009Solver","header":"6.3. More check-sat functions","id":"/Solver/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Solver--More-check-sat-functions"}});